WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:01.860
- We are doing a very special round

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:04.280
of Drew's Crew and A.

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:05.820
So, let's get into it.

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:09.100
First step is a question right
here in our studio audience.

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:13.229
It's Yvette Atiles and I would
love to know your question.

00:00:13.229 --> 00:00:14.880
- Hi Drew, hi Mikel.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:18.434
So I'm in the process of
purchasing my new home.

00:00:18.434 --> 00:00:19.580
- [Drew] Congratulations.

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:22.020
- And as you know, it's very expensive.

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:26.160
So my question is, what
furniture should I be thinking

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:30.160
about investing and what
furniture should I sort of think

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:33.490
about buying in the cheaper end of it?

00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:34.860
- So, here's the thing with that.

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:38.100
Typically you want to spend
the big bucks on the things

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:40.450
or the surfaces that you're
gonna touch the most,

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:44.580
i.e. like your bed, your
sofa, your coffee table,

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:47.080
things that you're gonna
have a lot of high traffic.

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:48.870
Now you can kick back a little bit

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:52.860
on smaller things like
bookcases, floating shelves,

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:54.810
things that people aren't
gonna touch as much.

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:58.950
It's a matter of finding things
that are not so expensive,

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:01.810
but functional and if it's like crappy,

00:01:01.810 --> 00:01:04.627
keep it somewhere up hidden so
nobody even sees that thing.

00:01:04.627 --> 00:01:07.161
- Okay, thank you Yvette
for your question.

00:01:07.161 --> 00:01:09.350
(audience applauds)

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:11.250
All right next is a social media question

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:14.330
from Rosalia Flores on Facebook,

00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:16.924
and she asks spring cleaning my makeup.

00:01:16.924 --> 00:01:20.650
What should I keep in my
go to arsenal for makeup

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:24.440
and how should I know to edit?

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:27.360
- Oh, we love a makeup edit, right Drew?

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:28.193
We love this.

00:01:28.193 --> 00:01:29.690
Well, the thing is you want
something minimal, right?

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:31.040
You don't want loads
and loads of products.

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:33.990
So for that, you wanna think
about multitasking products.

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:37.020
Number one, I would say
for, the spring is coming.

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:41.400
You want something that is a
tinted moisturizer with an SPF.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:44.510
You want your skin to look
polished every day, not overdone.

00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:47.170
You want people to basically
compliment your skin,

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:48.449
not your makeup.

00:01:48.449 --> 00:01:50.957
The second thing I would say for spring

00:01:50.957 --> 00:01:54.020
is a bright color eyeshadow, right?

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:58.020
We are seeing that all over
as a big, big trend coming up,

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:00.930
blue, coral, green, anything you, I mean,

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:03.120
it could be a line or it
could be a smudge on the eye,

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:04.420
something bright and bold.

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:05.253
- [Drew] I love that.

00:02:05.253 --> 00:02:06.231
- Right.

00:02:06.231 --> 00:02:08.970
- I think go to the Milk Makeup website.

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:11.410
It's my favorite company.

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:13.940
RMS makes a great little mini palette

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:17.890
that I'm obsessed with that
has a highliner and a bronzer

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:19.768
and a little lip and cheek.

00:02:19.768 --> 00:02:20.601
- Yep.

00:02:20.601 --> 00:02:24.150
- And one thing I've realized

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:28.698
is that I have usually bought
a lot of my products in bulk.

00:02:28.698 --> 00:02:30.550
Big mistake.

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:31.410
Don't do it.

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:33.690
I've had to throw out a few things

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:36.280
because they went past the two year mark.

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:40.900
And so I would also keep
it a minimalist approach

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:43.840
when you're buying your
products and cleansers,

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:47.320
skin care and makeup, you can
always go out and buy another.

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:49.910
But the fact that I have
a few that are expired,

00:02:49.910 --> 00:02:52.430
breaks my heart and makes
me feel so wasteful.

00:02:52.430 --> 00:02:55.390
- You would rather your
product run out than it go off.

00:02:55.390 --> 00:02:56.550
- [Drew] Yes, exactly.

00:02:56.550 --> 00:02:58.170
- Save the bulk buying
for the toilet roll.

00:02:58.170 --> 00:02:59.340
- Agree.

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:00.173
(everyone laughing)

00:03:00.173 --> 00:03:03.530
And for that, I have a
new bamboo toilet paper

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:06.858
that took me a minute to get
used to but I'm all for it now.

00:03:06.858 --> 00:03:07.691
- [Zanna] Ooh Really?

00:03:07.691 --> 00:03:08.524
- [Ross] Do tell.

00:03:08.524 --> 00:03:11.990
- It saves trees and paper
towels and toilet paper.

00:03:11.990 --> 00:03:14.650
And they it's completely sustainable.

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:15.483
No tree.

00:03:15.483 --> 00:03:17.042
- Does it save your bum?

00:03:17.042 --> 00:03:18.410
- It's a.

00:03:18.410 --> 00:03:19.243
(audience applauds)

00:03:19.243 --> 00:03:22.212
- Again, it took me a
second to get used to.

00:03:22.212 --> 00:03:23.770
- I have the toilet paper too

00:03:23.770 --> 00:03:25.540
and you know, she's a sturdy paper.

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:27.280
If you know what I mean.

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:30.960
- She's not as, she
doesn't feel like velvet

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:32.794
but she's not killing trees.

00:03:32.794 --> 00:03:33.627
- [Ross] Exactly.

00:03:33.627 --> 00:03:34.682
- So, if I to take one for the team.

00:03:34.682 --> 00:03:35.515
(audience applauds)

00:03:35.515 --> 00:03:36.348
- [Zanna] Amazing.

00:03:36.348 --> 00:03:37.561
- I'm going to.

00:03:37.561 --> 00:03:39.132
Thank you Ozalia so much.

00:03:39.132 --> 00:03:41.715
(upbeat music)

